			<div class="content" id="post-content-4819708">
									

<p>Got a PM about this, took a quick look. I'll be honest, I'm iffy on this because it seems tailor-made to be a headache for the Foundation, and seems at face value more authorial meddling than narrative-driven plot points.</p>
<p>Aside from facilitating the typical &quot;wow this is so unusually inconveniently difficult to contain&quot; approach, why does this person exhibit this anomaly? Why is it that out of all people who could have been affected, it just <em>happens</em> to be a criminal who has this blood monster summoning ability? What happens if the person is just dropped off in the middle of nowhere with no barriers? Even if they don't want to be there, what can the blood monster do?</p>
<p>With regards to the Foundation response, there are a lot of alternative responses they could have used that would make them look less incompetent (offering a janitorial position to someone who has no preexisting qualifications or experience for the role, no Foundation loyalty, and no guarantee to remain in such a role is a huge oversight). Why didn't they amnesticize the guy and make him think that he's some sort of hermit? Or just put him in a luxury suite, then sedate him/put him in a medically induced coma? Or just hold him in an area that prevents the blood humanoid from forming properly (e.g., an industrial freezer)?</p>
<p>I feel like if you want to make this work, you need a stronger story-based reason for this entity to exist the way it does, beyond just &quot;I want to write about a thing that is hard to contain&quot;. Otherwise, the worldbuilding falls flat, and the setup feels contrived and difficult to take seriously.</p>

							</div>

								
					
